#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105573 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105715 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf98ee24-7774-5674-8a1e-63f4f6d225c0" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function"}] :question/id #uuid "625c532a-d455-5b15-880d-e9fc5ae8ee72" :question/math-academy-id "e-374" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int \\left(\\dfrac 2 x\\right)\\, \\textrm{d} x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $2$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\dfrac 1 x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{2}{x})\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105574 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105716 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c865ec2-5bed-5892-966b-a586097be923" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating the Sum of a Reciprocal Function and a Power Function"}] :question/id #uuid "9c57c69e-efa2-5e82-992c-53570e61af09" :question/math-academy-id "e-375" :question/problem "Find the integral of $\\dfrac 1 {2x} + 3x$ with respect to $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{1}{2x}+3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int 3x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}ln⁡|x|+3\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{1+1}}{1+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{ln⁡|x|}{2}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105575 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105720 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df7dddc6-a4fd-5b1b-9b73-8528e5224ca9" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant Using the Laws of Logarithms"}] :question/id #uuid "bf47e35e-da86-5f88-b1e7-0cc731c37b31" :question/math-academy-id "e-376" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of $\\dfrac {3+2x} {x} ?$" :question/worked-solution "We separate the integrand into the sum of two fractions so that we can simplify each term. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3+2x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{2x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{3}{x}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3ln⁡|x|+2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C =3 \\ln K$ where $K$ is some other constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $3\\ln|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3+2x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3ln⁡|x|+2x+C \\\\  & =3ln⁡|x|+2x+3ln⁡K \\\\  & =3ln⁡(K|x|)+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238029 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105716 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c865ec2-5bed-5892-966b-a586097be923" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating the Sum of a Reciprocal Function and a Power Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4abe304b-eb2b-5d95-b662-98795ce766a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{3/2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236297} {:answer/id #uuid "6d00a003-0364-5b3a-b73d-cf2606acaf82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{5/2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236506} {:answer/id #uuid "b7b12b3f-b8dd-53cd-a79b-4f8d64e6c135" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}{x}^{5/2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236984} {:answer/id #uuid "c71fc3c7-fa6d-5f20-8988-65604e335a7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3/2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237078} {:answer/id #uuid "ca4ffb28-67bb-5e22-a3dc-17c627b249f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{5/2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237101}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7b12b3f-b8dd-53cd-a79b-4f8d64e6c135" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}{x}^{5/2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236984} :answer-field/id #uuid "67ce5cc2-6611-553c-af07-6c3510863b9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1a6388af-23c9-54c5-98cc-a8fb2fcf5235" :question/math-academy-id "q-103593" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int ({x}^{3/2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1})\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the sum rule and get\n\n$$\n\\int ({x}^{3/2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1})\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {x}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nNow, using the power rule in the first term, and taking out the coefficient in the second term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{3/2+1}}{\\frac{3}{2}+1}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}{x}^{5/2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573909 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105720 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df7dddc6-a4fd-5b1b-9b73-8528e5224ca9" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant Using the Laws of Logarithms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f37b82c-5fa6-5b06-b248-ea8614b89da8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|K|x)-\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573544} {:answer/id #uuid "9667b0ce-bbf4-5b73-a83f-407769162c58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|x|+K)-\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573620} {:answer/id #uuid "9c782528-b84f-5779-ad29-e6d25d867a9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|x|)-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573626} {:answer/id #uuid "a7508ae4-4668-52ac-9837-4d44beeb8f5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|x|)-\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573640} {:answer/id #uuid "e834c180-cd3a-5f45-a350-e6123e54e728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|x|+K)-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573731}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7508ae4-4668-52ac-9837-4d44beeb8f5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|x|)-\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573640} :answer-field/id #uuid "1227ed44-e539-58ab-a920-6350e880868c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a8ac5ab9-a2e7-5a8d-9104-00d51ece29df" :question/math-academy-id "q-29840" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of $(\\frac{10}{x}-\\sqrt[3]{x})?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the sum and constant factor rules and take the coefficients out of the integral. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{10}{x}-\\sqrt[3]{x})\\text{d}x & =10\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int {x}^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\frac{{x}^{1/3+1}}{(\\frac{1}{3}+1)}+C \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{4/3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=10\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $10\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{10}{x}-\\sqrt[3]{x})\\text{d}x & =10\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}+10\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =10(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K)-\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|x|)-\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106280 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105716 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c865ec2-5bed-5892-966b-a586097be923" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating the Sum of a Reciprocal Function and a Power Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8140037d-2d2c-5542-a0ba-9edf6b7bbf28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\ln \\mid x \\mid - \\frac{2}{x} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103829} {:answer/id #uuid "0405b19e-98fb-5a56-97e4-d2d44340c6ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln \\mid x \\mid - x^{1/2} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103830} {:answer/id #uuid "14506e14-75d8-5464-ba2e-4e8d416dcb89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x - 2x^{1/2} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103831} {:answer/id #uuid "3737381e-42d1-5ec5-a0be-6e2c2c114442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\ln \\mid x \\mid - 2x^{1/2} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103832} {:answer/id #uuid "72930a31-c1dd-529c-95f0-0c9ed79b8c26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln \\mid x \\mid + 2x^{1/2} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103833}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f13e7011-de56-51ec-9c85-6d23ec9e1c7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "60e355f2-c5a8-565b-92ee-4e1844ef9c98" :question/math-academy-id "q-29857" :question/problem "Find the integral of $\\frac{2}{x} - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}$ with respect to $x$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573896 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105715 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf98ee24-7774-5674-8a1e-63f4f6d225c0" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67f74e0e-5f1b-51c7-ba2a-870910fcf5bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573551} {:answer/id #uuid "7298b1bf-ca7a-5d14-a64c-391d2cf1b72b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3{x}^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573568} {:answer/id #uuid "8859334f-13b0-51e5-bf03-c10268ba8807" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573600} {:answer/id #uuid "9d57d003-4898-5a03-9c43-5018e3b9934d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{|x|}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573627} {:answer/id #uuid "a7d9d531-25bc-5c10-8238-cd5011735ae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7d9d531-25bc-5c10-8238-cd5011735ae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573642} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4bd31d5-cd9b-5809-9466-5a14c329db97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "73a6be96-270e-560a-af2f-74b977e32a10" :question/math-academy-id "q-49065" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{3x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $\\frac{1}{3}$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{3x} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573922 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105720 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df7dddc6-a4fd-5b1b-9b73-8528e5224ca9" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant Using the Laws of Logarithms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a9e1968-ebda-5b8a-87f8-c6f794e88a9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{4}{x}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573436} {:answer/id #uuid "1c759ace-fa8b-5d0e-a8eb-8ecf03fcdc4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{2}{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573455} {:answer/id #uuid "33746465-e717-5154-b425-3b9867de6dcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573490} {:answer/id #uuid "41ec2527-87bc-5b8c-82c1-12de45de080a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|1-\\frac{2}{x}|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573506} {:answer/id #uuid "c6661073-9fee-582c-ad8c-e2b7b6a4e998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573680}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a9e1968-ebda-5b8a-87f8-c6f794e88a9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{4}{x}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573436} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc9d886d-2453-589c-b70a-85755f09affc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c14c2cc2-7d97-5dae-839b-1375800e6971" :question/math-academy-id "q-49101" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of ${(1-\\frac{2}{x})}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Expanding the binomial and applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1-\\frac{2}{x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int (1-\\frac{4}{x}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{4}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x-4\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+4\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+4\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{-2+1}}{-2+1}+C \\\\  & =x-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\frac{4}{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1-\\frac{2}{x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =x-\\frac{4}{x}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =x-\\frac{4}{x}-4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K) \\\\  & =x-\\frac{4}{x}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339052 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105715 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf98ee24-7774-5674-8a1e-63f4f6d225c0" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f88c79e-e25c-5fcd-a473-3f9adb3d4e9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{7}ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336327} {:answer/id #uuid "58b493c2-73d3-5db1-af15-0200a2937632" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{ln⁡|x|}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336844} {:answer/id #uuid "daf20704-2d65-5753-af90-f6e026c52e12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{7}ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337940} {:answer/id #uuid "ec4261ce-b5b3-5551-9fbd-56937e1eea24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{\\sqrt{7}}ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338091} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a97ce9-ff10-5758-b496-1ffe87d0bb3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-ln⁡|\\sqrt{7}x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f88c79e-e25c-5fcd-a473-3f9adb3d4e9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{7}ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336327} :answer-field/id #uuid "f930863c-55bb-55cc-bd9d-51b8ae4abea3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d9daecf1-e255-5c0c-a433-fdc798f551aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-72059" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{7}}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{7}$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{7}ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
